1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,286
Five common mistakes.

2
00:00:01,286 --> 00:00:03,857
These come up over and over.

3
00:00:03,857 --> 00:00:06,000
If you can avoid them,

4
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:10,714
your prompts will land in the top quartile of what most

5
00:00:10,714 --> 00:00:11,914
people produce.

6
00:00:11,914 --> 00:00:14,057
One — being too vague.

7
00:00:14,057 --> 00:00:18,771
"Write something about marketing." That's not a prompt, that's a topic.

8
00:00:18,771 --> 00:00:23,486
Replace it with: "Write a 500-word blog post about email marketing

9
00:00:23,486 --> 00:00:25,629
best practices for small businesses,

10
00:00:25,629 --> 00:00:30,343
aimed at owners with no marketing background." Now the AI has

11
00:00:30,343 --> 00:00:32,914
a target it can actually hit.

12
00:00:32,914 --> 00:00:34,629
Two — assuming context.

13
00:00:34,629 --> 00:00:37,200
"Fix this code." Where's the code?

14
00:00:37,200 --> 00:00:39,343
"Improve this paragraph." Which paragraph?

15
00:00:39,343 --> 00:00:42,771
You can't reference content the AI can't see.

16
00:00:42,771 --> 00:00:47,486
Always paste in the actual material you're asking it to work

17
00:00:47,486 --> 00:00:48,686
on.

18
00:00:48,686 --> 00:00:50,400
Or summarize it explicitly.

19
00:00:50,400 --> 00:00:52,114
Three — ignoring format.

20
00:00:52,114 --> 00:00:56,829
If you don't say what shape you want the answer in,

21
00:00:56,829 --> 00:01:00,257
you'll get whatever shape the model defaults to.

22
00:01:00,257 --> 00:01:01,543
Specify: bullet points.

23
00:01:01,543 --> 00:01:02,743
Numbered list.

24
00:01:02,743 --> 00:01:03,943
Paragraphs.

25
00:01:03,943 --> 00:01:05,143
Table.

26
00:01:05,143 --> 00:01:06,343
Markdown headers.

27
00:01:06,343 --> 00:01:07,543
JSON.

28
00:01:07,543 --> 00:01:12,257
The format isn't an afterthought — it's part of the deliverable.

29
00:01:12,257 --> 00:01:13,971
Four — not iterating.

30
00:01:13,971 --> 00:01:17,400
The first response is rarely the best one.

31
00:01:17,400 --> 00:01:19,543
Treat it as a draft.

32
00:01:19,543 --> 00:01:20,743
Refine.

33
00:01:20,743 --> 00:01:21,943
Tighten.

34
00:01:21,943 --> 00:01:23,229
Reroll a section.

35
00:01:23,229 --> 00:01:27,086
Iteration is the work — it's not extra effort,

36
00:01:27,086 --> 00:01:28,800
it's the actual job.

37
00:01:28,800 --> 00:01:30,086
Five — overcomplicating.

38
00:01:30,086 --> 00:01:31,371
The opposite mistake.

39
00:01:31,371 --> 00:01:36,086
A 500-word prompt with thirteen constraints and four examples is harder

40
00:01:36,086 --> 00:01:40,371
to debug than a 100-word prompt that gets it right.

41
00:01:40,371 --> 00:01:42,086
Be concise but complete.

42
00:01:42,086 --> 00:01:45,086
Cut anything that doesn't earn its place.

43
00:01:45,086 --> 00:01:49,800
If you're not sure whether a sentence in your prompt is

44
00:01:49,800 --> 00:01:52,800
helping, delete it and see what happens.

45
00:01:52,800 --> 00:01:57,514
The checklist on screen is the version you'll see in the

46
00:01:57,514 --> 00:02:01,371
workbook — before, during, after, and for production use.

47
00:02:01,371 --> 00:02:06,086
I want to add three more habits you won't see on

48
00:02:06,086 --> 00:02:07,286
the slide.

49
00:02:07,286 --> 00:02:09,000
These are about safety.

50
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:12,429
First — don't paste sensitive data into prompts.

51
00:02:12,429 --> 00:02:13,629
Customer names.

52
00:02:13,629 --> 00:02:14,829
Internal credentials.

53
00:02:14,829 --> 00:02:16,029
Unreleased pricing.

54
00:02:16,029 --> 00:02:17,229
Private documents.

55
00:02:17,229 --> 00:02:18,514
Personal health information.

56
00:02:18,514 --> 00:02:23,229
AI providers may use prompt content for evaluation or model improvement,

57
00:02:23,229 --> 00:02:26,229
depending on your plan and your settings.

58
00:02:26,229 --> 00:02:30,943
Treat your prompt like a public message until you've confirmed otherwise.

59
00:02:30,943 --> 00:02:34,800
If a prompt would be embarrassing to see leaked,

60
00:02:34,800 --> 00:02:39,514
find another way to do the task — strip identifying details,

61
00:02:39,514 --> 00:02:43,371
swap in fake data, or use a self-hosted model.

62
00:02:43,371 --> 00:02:45,514
Second — verify factual claims.

63
00:02:45,514 --> 00:02:46,800
AI models hallucinate.

64
00:02:46,800 --> 00:02:51,514
Names, dates, statistics, citations, court cases, product specifications, version numbers —

65
00:02:51,514 --> 00:02:55,371
they can sound completely confident, and be completely wrong.

66
00:02:55,371 --> 00:03:00,086
Anything you'll put your name on, double-check from a primary source.

67
00:03:00,086 --> 00:03:04,800
The rule of thumb I use: if a customer or boss

68
00:03:04,800 --> 00:03:07,800
could ask "where did this come from?",

69
00:03:07,800 --> 00:03:11,657
you should know the answer before you hit send.

70
00:03:11,657 --> 00:03:15,943
Third — be aware of where the model's expertise ends.

71
00:03:15,943 --> 00:03:18,943
Models are pattern matchers, not domain experts.

72
00:03:18,943 --> 00:03:22,800
They're great for first drafts, brainstorming, and structured tasks.

73
00:03:22,800 --> 00:03:27,514
They're not a substitute for legal advice, medical advice, financial decisions,

74
00:03:27,514 --> 00:03:30,943
or anything where being wrong has real consequences.

75
00:03:30,943 --> 00:03:33,943
The further you push outside common patterns,

76
00:03:33,943 --> 00:03:37,800
the more carefully you need to check the output.

77
00:03:37,800 --> 00:03:39,514
And for high-stakes domains,

78
00:03:39,514 --> 00:03:44,229
the model should be a research assistant — not the decision-maker.

79
00:03:44,229 --> 00:03:48,943
Those three habits — don't share sensitive data, verify factual claims,

80
00:03:48,943 --> 00:03:51,086
know the model's edges —

81
00:03:51,086 --> 00:03:54,943
are how working prompt engineers stay out of trouble.

82
00:03:54,943 --> 00:03:58,800
Pair them with the technique playbook from earlier sessions,

83
00:03:58,800 --> 00:04:02,229
and you've got a workflow you can trust.

84
00:04:02,229 --> 00:04:06,514
In the final session, we'll wrap up — quiz, workbook,

85
00:04:06,514 --> 00:04:08,657
and what comes next. ---
